# Windows Desktop (GUI)

- GUI stands for Graphical User Interface.
- GUI is the screen shown after logging into Windows.
- Login requires username and password credentials.
- GUI makes interaction with Windows easy through icons and menus.

## Main Components of Windows Desktop

1. Desktop
2. Start Menu
3. Search Box (Cortana)
4. Task View
5. Taskbar
6. Toolbars
7. Notification Area

# Desktop

- Desktop contains shortcuts to programs, files, and folders.
- Icons can be organized or scattered on the desktop.
- Right-clicking on desktop opens a context menu.
- Context menu options:
  - View
  - Sort by
  - Refresh
  - New
- Desktop settings can be customized.

## Display Setting

- Used to change:
  - Screen resolution
  - Screen orientation
  - Scaling/layout
- Multiple monitors can also be configured.
- In Remote Desktop sessions, some display settings are disabled.

## Personalize Settings

- Used to change:
  - Wallpaper/background
  - Themes
  - Fonts
  - Colors

# Start Menu

- Start Menu gives access to:
  - Apps/programs
  - Files
  - Utility tools
  - Settings
- Opened by clicking the Windows logo.

## Sections of Start Menu

### 1. Left Section

- Contains quick shortcuts.
- Includes:
  - User account
  - Documents
  - Pictures
  - Settings
  - Power options

### 2. Middle Section

- Shows:
  - Recently added applications
  - Installed applications
- Programs are arranged alphabetically.
- Alphabet grid helps quickly jump to apps.

### 3. Right Section (Tiles)

- Contains app tiles/icons.
- Tiles can be:
  - Resized
  - Unpinned
  - Customized
- Apps can be pinned to Start Menu.

# Taskbar

- Located at the bottom of the desktop.
- Contains:
  - Running applications
  - Pinned applications
  - Search box
  - Task View button
- Right-clicking taskbar opens customization options.
- Taskbar settings allow enabling/disabling components.

## Taskbar Features

- Hovering over app icon shows preview thumbnail.
- Open applications appear on taskbar.
- Closed applications disappear unless pinned.

# Notification Area

- Located at bottom-right corner of screen.
- Displays:
  - Date and time
  - Network icon
  - Volume icon
  - Action Center
- Icons can be customized from Taskbar settings.

# Windows File System

- Modern Windows uses NTFS (New Technology File System).
- Older file systems:
  - FAT16/FAT32
  - HPFS

## Features of NTFS

- Supports files larger than 4GB.
- Provides file and folder permissions.
- Supports compression.
- Supports encryption using EFS.
- Uses journaling for recovery after failures.

# Remote Desktop

- Remote Desktop allows accessing another computer remotely.
- Requires:
  - Machine IP
  - Username
  - Password
- Uses RDP (Remote Desktop Protocol).

# Important Notes

- Search box can be hidden from taskbar.
- Task View button can also be disabled.
- Action Center is part of Notification Area.
- Right-click menus provide extra management options.
